          "path": "_m/g/c/p/v/kensington-palace-wp/exhibit-kensington-pal.jpg",
          "filename": "Floor standing clock, by Louis-Franc Roubiliac, 1730-1743, wood, gilt metal, silver, paint - Kensington Palace - London, England - DSC03895.jpg",
          "caption": "Exhibit in Kensington Palace - London, England. Design by Louis-François Roubiliac with painted allegorical scenes by Amigoni and applied silver bas-reliefs by Rysbrack, mechanism by Charles Clay, completed by John Pyke. It was evidently a musical clock, described in a newspaper article, 1743, as follows: \"The Musick consists of an agreeable Variety of Pieces, composed by the three great Masters Geminiani, Handel and Corelli; and properly adapted to the machine by mr Geminiani. It performs not only in Concert, but alternatively on several Instruments, in a most surprising manner, exceeding the Performance of the best Hands.\"",
          "alt": "Exhibit in Kensington Palace - London, England. Design by Louis-François Roubiliac with painted allegorical scenes by Amigoni and applied silver bas-reliefs by Rysbrack, mechanism by Charles Clay, completed by John Pyke. It was evidently a musical clock, described in a newspaper article, 1743, as follows: \"The Musick consists of an agreeable Variety of Pieces, composed by the three great Masters Geminiani, Handel and Corelli; and properly adapted to the machine by mr Geminiani. It performs not only in Concert, but alternatively on several Instruments, in a most surprising manner, exceeding the Performance of the best Hands.\"",
